{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"Operator decision recorded — bridge NOT resumed, and why.\n\nStanding rule from the operator: resume a paused bridge only if the pause is older than 24 hours; otherwise assume another session owns it.\n\nMeasured at decision time:\n\n    paused_at  2026-09-05T04:32:38Z\n    now        2026-09-05T05:00:24Z\n    age        27.8 minutes (0.46 h)  ->  older than 24h: FALSE\n\nSo the pause is recent and presumed owned by a concurrent session's `repair:doctor:aca7242c-cd02-411b-931e-37b385f375a7`. Not resumed.\n\nThis is worth recording rather than leaving implicit, because it exposes the operational gap this ticket is about: the ONLY reason a 24-hour rule is applicable here is that `paused_at` happened to be visible in a workflow LOG LINE. `rebar bridge status` returned `\"pause\": null` against a stale pass record minutes later, so had that run not printed `BRIDGE_PAUSED`, the pause age would not have been answerable at all — and the rule could not have been applied.\n\nThat strengthens the third acceptance criterion: `bridge status` must report the CURRENT pause state including its age, because an ownership rule keyed on pause age is unusable without it."},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1788584441524746001,"uuid":"1d5568fa-af4a-4673-886c-8c90bddd3058"}